<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brocéliande</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brocéliande is a legendary forest of Arthurian romance, first placed in Brittany by the poet Wace in the twelfth century. It is linked with the fountain of Barenton, the fairy Viviane and the enchanter Merlin. Since the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it has been identified with the forest of Paimpont, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>, which draws many visitors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/broceliande.jpg" alt="Brocéliande" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idylls_of_the_King_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The forest was earlier known as Brécheliant and Brécilien, and the etymology is uncertain. The oldest form, Brecheliant, may join the Celtic Brec'h, hill, to a man's name; the later Brocéliande, first found in the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chretien-de-troyes">Chrétien de Troyes</a>, may come from bro, country in Breton, Cornish and Welsh. A reference to Briscelim sinus Armoricus occurs in the Cosmographia of Bernardus Silvestris, read to Pope Eugene III in 1147–48.</p><p>In his Roman de Rou of about 1160, Wace counted among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> those of Brecheliant, about whom many legends were told. He described the fountain of Barenton, where hunters wet a stone with its water to summon rain, and reported rumours of fairies. Wace went to Brittany in search of these wonders and came back disappointed, writing that he had sought marvels and found none.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In Chrétien's Le Chevalier au lion of the 1170s, Yvain pours water from a spring onto a stone in Brocéliande and raises a storm that summons the knight Esclados le Ros. Robert de Boron first tied Merlin to the forest in his poem Merlin, and the prose Vulgate Cycle set there episodes of Merlin and Viviane and the Val sans retour, where Morgan le Fay trapped unfaithful knights until Lancelot freed them. The forest's unusual weather is noted by Giraldus Cambrensis, Alexandre Neckham and William the Breton.</p><p>Modern writers returned to it, among them Alfred, Lord Tennyson in Idylls of the King, Jean Lorrain in his play Brocéliande of 1898, where Viviane shuts Merlin inside an oak, and Robert Holdstock in Merlin's Wood. The name inspired J.R.R. Tolkien's Beleriand, called Broseliand in early sketches of The Silmarillion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Early sources give unclear or conflicting locations. Since the 15th century, following Jean Cabaret d'Orville, some have linked Brocéliande to the forest of Lorge near Quintin. Some scholars think it never existed. Jean Markale regarded it as a remnant of the forest that covered central Brittany until the High Middle Ages, and William A. Young has argued for equating it with the forest of Celython, the Great Wood of Caledon, from tales of the Brythonic kingdoms beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brocéliande” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Breton storytellers helped carry the Matter of Britain into French literature: Marie de France presented her twelfth-century lais as Breton tales, and the forest of <b>Brocéliande</b> became a setting of Arthurian romance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
